;; Which boxes this repository knows how to build a node stick for, and for
;; each field, whether anyone has actually read it off the machine.
;;
;; Why this file exists. ADR-0208/0210 and the node-agent tranche built the
;; installer; the commit that landed it says plainly that it was kept "because
;; the box exists and the means to make another one did not". The means that
;; were missing are not code -- `make-node-autoinstall-iso.cljs` is already
;; machine-agnostic -- they are the ARGUMENTS. Which hostname, which disk may
;; be erased, which console door, which tailnet. Those lived in one session's
;; shell history and nowhere else, so the second box had to rediscover them.
;;
;; The second box is the reason for the file: an AMD Ryzen 5 6600HS board
;; (ADR-0214). Writing its arguments down forced the question this file is
;; really about -- which of them are MEASURED and which are guesses that happen
;; to be typed confidently. A stick built from a guessed disk serial installs
;; unattended onto whatever disk happened to match, which on a box someone
;; cared about is the one failure this whole chain exists to prevent.
;;
;; So every box-specific field carries its own provenance, and the driver
;; (`scripts/make-node-installer.cljs`) refuses to spend an unverified field as
;; though it were measured. A field with no record is :unverified, which is NOT
;; the same as absent and NOT the same as false -- it means nobody looked.
{:format :aiueos.node-machines/v1

 ;; The vocabulary, small on purpose. Three states, and the middle one is the
 ;; one that gets lost: "the owner told me" is real information and is not a
 ;; measurement, and collapsing it into either neighbour loses something.
 :provenance
 {:measured     "read off this machine, by a named command, on a named date"
  :owner-stated "the owner said so; nobody has read it off the box"
  :unverified   "nobody has looked. Not absent, not false -- unmeasured."}

 ;; Defaults, so a machine entry says only what differs. A default is a
 ;; decision too: `noble` is the codename the tailscale apt repository is
 ;; addressed by, so it tracks the ISO rather than being free text.
 :defaults
 {:endpoint         "https://murakumo.cloud"
  :username         "aiueos"
  :ubuntu-codename  "noble"
  :iso-architecture :amd64
  :storage          :interactive}   ; a human picks the disk unless a serial is measured

 ;; What the installer needs per box, and which of those are allowed to be
 ;; unverified. The driver reads this rather than restating it: one list of
 ;; required fields, in one place, or the two drift.
 :required
 {:always   [:hostname :ssh-key]
  :for-unattended-storage [:disk-serial]}

 :machines

 ;; ── the new box, and the reason this tranche exists ─────────────────────
 {:amd-6600hs
  {:hostname "aiueos-6600hs"
   :facts
   {:cpu {:value "AMD Ryzen 5 6600H with Radeon Graphics" :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-11" :by "aiueos-node-hwprobe sha256:d9a5c9f0002a"
          ;; MEASURED 2026-09-11, and it corrected the owner's statement: the
          ;; chip is a 6600H, not a 6600HS. The machine id and hostname still
          ;; say 6600hs and are NOT being renamed -- a name is a discovery
          ;; alias, the measured value is the fact, and churning the id would
          ;; break the stick already built against it.
          ;;
          ;; The conclusion it was recorded for survives the correction: 6600H
          ;; is Rembrandt and the K16's 7735HS is Rembrandt-R, the same silicon
          ;; rebadged. Same family 19h, same Zen 3+ core, same RDNA2 gfx1035
          ;; display block.
          ;;
          ;; The actionable consequence is a NEGATIVE one, and it is the whole
          ;; reason to write it down: this CPU change justifies no new kernel
          ;; argument, no new firmware package, and no new driver. If a boot
          ;; here fails in a way the K16 did not, the cause is the BOARD -- its
          ;; NIC, its BIOS, its disk controller mode -- not the CPU. Do not
          ;; spend a debugging session on amd_pstate because the number
          ;; changed.
          :same-silicon-as :gmktec-k16}

    ;; Everything below is box-specific and nobody has looked. They are listed
    ;; rather than omitted, because an absent key reads as "not applicable" and
    ;; these are all applicable and all unknown.
    :board       {:value "LXY MQ" :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-11" :by "aiueos-node-hwprobe sha256:d9a5c9f0002a"}
    :nic         {:value "r8169 [10ec:8168], rtw89_8851be [10ec:b851]" :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-11" :by "aiueos-node-hwprobe sha256:d9a5c9f0002a"
                  ;; This is the field that can stop an install, and the only
                  ;; one the Ubuntu route can be relaxed about. The bespoke
                  ;; initramfs had to carry a driver and lost (an RTL8125
                  ;; wanting firmware that is not upstream); Ubuntu's installer
                  ;; carries r8169, igc, e1000e and the rest already. So an
                  ;; unknown NIC is not a blocker to BUILDING the stick.
                  ;;
                  ;; It is a blocker to two later things, and they are worth
                  ;; separating: the autoinstall's late-commands reach the
                  ;; network (tailscale's apt repository), so a NIC Ubuntu
                  ;; cannot drive turns into a late-command failure rather than
                  ;; a clear "no network" -- and the node is not a node until
                  ;; it can heartbeat.
                  :measure-by "the hw-record the install itself writes; see :hw-record below"}
    :disk-model  {:value "YXF800E3-256GT" :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-11" :by "aiueos-node-hwprobe sha256:d9a5c9f0002a"}
    :disk-serial {:value "2025010918756132" :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-11" :by "aiueos-node-hwprobe sha256:d9a5c9f0002a"
                  ;; The one field that changes the PRODUCT rather than the
                  ;; record. With it the stick installs unattended; without it
                  ;; the stick stops and asks a person which disk to erase.
                  ;; Defaulting this to "the only NVMe" is the refusal
                  ;; install-v1 already carries, for the reason it carries it.
                  :measure-by "lsblk -dno NAME,SERIAL,MODEL,SIZE at the box"}
    :nic-count   {:value 2 :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-11" :by "aiueos-node-hwprobe sha256:d9a5c9f0002a"
                  ;; Asked because the K16 turned out to have two, and the live
                  ;; node enrolled under `gmktec-k16-lan2` -- a name that only
                  ;; makes sense if you knew that. A second port is a naming
                  ;; decision before it is a networking one.
                  ;;
                  ;; ⚠ The two numbers here do NOT count the same thing, which
                  ;; was noticed only when the probe filled this one in. The
                  ;; K16's 2 is two ETHERNET ports, written by hand from
                  ;; ADR-0207. This 2 is "interfaces a driver bound to", and it
                  ;; is one ethernet plus one wifi -- read :nic, which names
                  ;; both drivers, rather than this number. Recorded rather than
                  ;; papered over: making the ingest count only ethernet would
                  ;; silently change the K16's meaning too, and that is a
                  ;; decision with its own evidence.
                  }}}

  ;; ── the board whose hardware facts this repo has actually measured ──────
  ;;
  ;; Kept here as the reference entry, because every fact in it was paid for.
  ;; Read the provenance: these are measurements of THE BOARD, made while the
  ;; bespoke installer was failing on it (the commit for the node-agent
  ;; tranche names all three).
  ;;
  ;; One thing this entry is NOT: a receipt for the box that was working on
  ;; 2026-09-10. That box's build left no committed receipt and this repository
  ;; does not record which board it was, so do not read this entry as "the
  ;; first Ubuntu node was the K16". Its hardware facts are measured; its
  ;; identity as that box is not.
  :gmktec-k16
  {:hostname "aiueos-k16-ubuntu"   ; deliberately NOT `gmktec-k16-lan2`: that
                                   ; name belongs to the native PXE relay's
                                   ; enrolment (ADR-0207) and pointing a second
                                   ; identity at it is the thing trust tiers
                                   ; exist to stop (409, name to another DID).
   :facts
   {:cpu         {:value "AMD Ryzen 7 7735HS" :provenance :measured
                  :on "2026-09-08" :by "contracts/physical-qualification-usb-v*.edn"}
    :board       {:value "GMKtec K16" :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-08"}
    :nic         {:value "Realtek RTL8125B" :provenance :measured
                  :on "2026-08-31"
                  :by "contracts/physical-persistent-worker-k16-v1.edn (observed MAC + revision case)"
                  :note "Its OPTIONAL firmware does not exist upstream at all,
                         which is what the hand-built initramfs died on. Ubuntu
                         drives it with r8169 and does not want that firmware."}
    :nic-count   {:value 2 :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-08"
                  :by "the live relay enrolled as gmktec-k16-lan2 (ADR-0207)"}
    :bios        {:value :acpi-table-wrong :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-10"
                  :note "Recorded as a property of this board, not of x86: one
                         of the three reasons the bespoke UKI route was
                         abandoned."}
    :uefi        {:value :refuses-hand-built-uki :provenance :measured :on "2026-09-10"
                  :note "Ubuntu's signed shim boots here; a hand-built UKI did
                         not. That asymmetry is why standing on a distribution
                         was the answer rather than a concession."}
    :disk-model  {:value nil :provenance :unverified}
    :disk-serial {:value nil :provenance :unverified}}}}

 ;; ── the probe the install performs on itself ────────────────────────────
 ;;
 ;; A new box has no measured fields, and the cheapest moment to measure it is
 ;; the one boot that is happening anyway. So the autoinstall writes a hardware
 ;; record into the installed system and prints it on the installer console.
 ;;
 ;; This is read-only and additive: it creates no new authority, changes no
 ;; decision, and is not on the critical path. If it fails the install does
 ;; not. That is deliberate -- a measurement that can break an install would be
 ;; bought at the price of the thing it measures.
 :hw-record
 {:written-by "scripts/make-node-autoinstall-iso.cljk (unless --no-hw-record)"
  :path "/var/log/aiueos-node-hwprobe.txt"
  :commands ["lscpu" "lspci -nn" "lsblk -dno NAME,SERIAL,MODEL,SIZE" "ip -o link"]
  :ingested-by "scripts/record-node-machine-probe.cljk --machine <id> --probe <file>"
  :rule "The ingest writes :measured only for fields the probe text actually
         contains, and refuses the whole file rather than writing nil for the
         rest. A partially-filled entry that claims :measured is worse than an
         unverified one, because the next reader stops asking."}}
